{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 270 --lines 85'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 270,\n  \"next_line\": 355,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"270: Local activation unit of Eq.(3) shares similar ideas with attention methods which are developed in NMT task(Bahdanau\\n271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\\\_goods\\\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n292: Take ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\\n293: Only parameters of non-zero sparse features appearing in each mini-batch needs to be updated in the scenario of SGD based optimization methods without regularization.\\n294: However, when adding ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization it needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters for each mini-batch, which leads to extremely heavy computations and is unacceptable with parameters scaling up to hundreds of millions.\\n295: Figure 3. Control function of PReLU and Dice.\\n296: In this paper, we introduce an efficient mini-batch aware regularizer, which only calculates the L2-norm over the parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch and makes the computation possible.\\n297: In fact, it is the embedding dictionary that contributes most of the parameters for CTR networks and arises the difficulty of heavy computation.\\n298: Let 𝐖∈ℝD×K\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K} denote parameters of the whole embedding dictionary, with DD as the dimensionality of the embedding vector and KK as the dimensionality of feature space.\\n299: Expand the ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization on 𝐖\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}} over samples\\n300: (4)\\n301: L2​(𝐖)=‖𝐖‖22=∑j=1K‖𝒘j‖22=∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮∑j=1KI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})&=\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\\\end{split}\\n302: where 𝒘j∈ℝD\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D} is the jj-th embedding vector,\\n303: I⁡(𝒙j≠0)I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0) denotes if the instance 𝒙\\\\bm{x} has the feature id jj, and njn_{j} denotes the number of occurrence for feature id jj in all samples. Eq.(4) can be transformed into Eq.(5) in the mini-batch aware manner\\n304: (5)\\n305: L2​(𝐖)=∑j=1K∑m=1B∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬmI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\n306: where BB denotes the number of mini-batches, ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m} denotes the mm-th mini-batch.\\n307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\\n309: Then Eq.(5) can be approximated by\\n310: (6)\\n311: L2​(𝐖)≈∑j=1K∑m=1Bαm​jnj​‖𝒘j‖22.L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})\\\\approx\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}.\\n312: In this way, we derive an approximated mini-batch aware version of ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization.\\n313: For the mm-th mini-batch, the gradient w.r.t. the embedding weights of feature jj is\\n314: (7)\\n315: 𝒘j←𝒘j−η⁡[1|ℬm|​∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬm∂L⁡(p⁡(𝒙),y)∂𝒘j+λ​αm​jnj​𝒘j],\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\leftarrow\\\\bm{w}_{j}-\\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}{\\\\frac{\\\\partial L(p(\\\\bm{x}),y)}{\\\\partial\\\\bm{w}_{j}}+\\\\lambda\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\bm{w}_{j}}\\\\right],\\n316: in which only parameters of features appearing in mm-th mini-batch participate in the computation of regularization.\\n317: 5.2. Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n318: PReLU (He\\n319: et al., 2015) is a commonly used activation function\\n320: (8)\\n321: f⁡(s)={s if ​s>0α​s if ​s≤0.=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,f(s)=\\\\begin{cases}s&\\\\text{ if }s>0\\\\\\\\\\n322: \\\\alpha s&\\\\text{ if }s\\\\leq 0.\\\\end{cases}=\\\\penalty\\\\ p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\n323: where ss is one dimension of the input of activation function f⁡(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) and p⁡(s)=I⁡(s>0)p(s)=I(s>0) is an indicator function which controls f⁡(s)f(s) to switch between two channels of f⁡(s)=sf(s)=s and f⁡(s)=α​sf(s)=\\\\alpha s. α\\\\alpha in the second channel is a learning parameter.\\n324: Here we refer to p⁡(s)p(s) as the control function. The left part of Fig.3 plots the control function of PReLU.\\n325: PReLU takes a hard rectified point with value of 00, which may be not suitable when the inputs of each layer follow different distributions.\\n326: Take this into consideration, we design a novel data adaptive activation function named Dice,\\n327: (9)\\n328: f⁡(s)=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,p⁡(s)=11+e−s−E⁡[s]V​a​r​[s]+ϵf(s)=p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\\\ \\\\ p(s)=\\\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\\\frac{s-E[s]}{\\\\sqrt{Var[s]+\\\\epsilon}}}}\\n329: with the control function to be plotted in the right part of Fig.3.\\n330: In the training phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is the mean and variance of input in each mini-batch.\\n331: In the testing phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is calculated by moving averages E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] over data.\\n332: ϵ\\\\epsilon is a small constant which is set to be 10−810^{-8} in our practice.\\n333: Dice can be viewed as a generalization of PReLu.\\n334: The key idea of Dice is to adaptively adjust the rectified point according to distribution of input data, whose value is set to be the mean of input.\\n335: Besides, Dice controls smoothly to switch between the two channels.\\n336: When E⁡(s)=0E(s)=0 and V​a​r​[s]=0Var[s]=0, Dice degenerates into PReLU.\\n337: 6. Experiments\\n338: In this section, we present our experiments in detail, including datasets, evaluation metric, experimental setup, model comparison and the corresponding analysis.\\n339: Experiments on two public datasets with user behaviors as well as a dataset collected from the display advertising system in Alibaba demonstrate the effectiveness of proposed approach which outperforms state-of-the-art methods on the CTR prediction task.\\n340: Both the public datasets and experiment codes are made available1.\\n341: 6.1. Datasets and Experimental Setup\\n342: Amazon Dataset22\\n343: 2\\n344: http://jmcauley.ucsd.edu/data/amazon/.\\n345: Amazon Dataset contains product reviews and metadata from Amazon, which is used as benchmark dataset(He and McAuley, 2016; Mcauley\\n346: et al., ; Veit\\n347: et al., 2015). We conduct experiments on a subset named Electronics, which contains 192,403 users, 63,001 goods, 801 categories and 1,689,188 samples. User behaviors in this dataset are rich, with more than 5 reviews for each users and goods. Features include goods_id, cate_id, user reviewed goods_id_list and cate_id_list. Let all behaviors of a user be (b1,b2,…,bk,…,bnb_{1},b_{2},\\\\ldots,b_{k},\\\\ldots,b_{n}), the task is to predict the (k+1)-th reviewed goods by making use of the first k reviewed goods.\\n348: Training dataset is generated with k=1,2,…,n−2k=1,2,\\\\ldots,n-2 for each user. In the test set, we predict the last one given the first n−1n-1 reviewed goods.\\n349: For all models, we use SGD as the optimizer with exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 1 and decay rate is set to 0.1. The mini-batch size is set to be 3232.\\n350: MovieLens Dataset33\\n351: 3\\n352: https://grouplens.org/datasets/movielens/20m/. MovieLens data(Harper and\\n353: Konstan, 2015) contains 138,493 users, 27,278 movies, 21 categories and 20,000,263 samples. To make it suitable for CTR prediction task, we transform it into a binary classification data. Original user rating of the movies is continuous value ranging from 0 to 5. We label the samples with rating of 4 and 5 to be positive and the rest to be negative. We segment the data into training and testing dataset based on userID. Among all 138,493 users, of which 100,000 are randomly selected into training set (about 14,470,000 samples) and the rest 38,493 into the test set (about 5,530,000 samples). The task is to predict whether user will rate a given movie to be above 3(positive label) based on historical behaviors. Features include movie_id, movie_cate_id and user rated movie_id_list, movie_cate_id_list. We use the same optimizer, learning rate and mini-batch size as described on Amazon Dataset.\\n354: Alibaba Dataset. We collected traffic logs from the online display advertising system in Alibaba, of which two weeks’ samples are used for training and samples of the following day for testing. The size of training and testing set is about 2 billions and 0.14 billion respectively. For all the deep models, the dimensionality of embedding vector is 12 for the whole 16 groups of features. Layers of MLP is set by 192×200×80×2192\\\\times 200\\\\times 80\\\\times 2. Due to the huge size of data, we set the mini-batch size to be 5000 and use Adam(Kingma and Ba, 2015) as the optimizer. We apply exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 0.001 and decay rate is set to 0.9.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
